#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Hicks Decomposition Recap]
// The title "Hicks Decomposition Recap" is shown on this slide
#v(40pt)
#only("2")[
#voiceover("Hicks Decomposition separates the effect of a price change into two components: the substitution effect and the income effect, while holding utility constant.")
]
#only("2-")[
- Separates price change effect into:
  - Substitution Effect 🔄
  - Income Effect 💰
- Holds utility constant 📈
]
// The key points are shown as bullet points from slide 2 onward
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Substitution Effect]
#v(40pt)
#only("1")[
#voiceover("The substitution effect shows how consumption changes when the price changes, keeping utility constant.")
]
#only("1-")[
- Shows change in consumption due to price change 📉
- Keeps utility constant 📈
]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Income Effect]
#v(40pt)
#only("1")[
#voiceover("The income effect shows how consumption changes due to a change in real income after adjusting for the substitution effect.")
]
#only("1-")[
- Shows change in consumption due to change in real income 💸
- Adjusts for substitution effect 🔄
]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Graphical Representation]
#v(40pt)
#only("1")[
#voiceover("Hicks Decomposition can be graphically represented using indifference curves and budget constraints to show the substitution and income effects.")
]
#only("1-")[
- Uses indifference curves 📈 and budget constraints 💰
- Shows substitution and income effects visually 📊
]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Mathematical Representation]
#v(40pt)
#only("1")[
#voiceover("Hicks Decomposition uses compensated demand functions to mathematically represent the substitution and income effects of a price change.")
]
#only("1-")[
- Uses compensated demand functions $h(p, U)$ 📜
- Mathematically represents substitution and income effects 🧮
]
]

